\label{lem:equiv-htpy-equiv}

A map $f \colon A \to B$ between $\infty$-categories in an $\infty$-cosmos $\ec{K}$ is an equivalence if and only if it extends to the data of a ``homotopy equivalence'' with the free-living isomorphism ${\BbbI}$ serving as the interval: that is, if there exist maps $g \colon B \to A$
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} & A & &  & B \\ A \arrow[ur, equals] \arrow[dr, "gf"'] \arrow[r, "\alpha"] & A^{\BbbI}   \arrow[u, two heads, "\sim", "\textup{ev}_0"'] \arrow[d, two heads, "\sim"',  "\textup{ev}_1"] & \text{and} &  B \arrow[dr, equals] \arrow[r, "\beta"] \arrow[ur, "fg"] & B^{\BbbI}  \arrow[u, two heads, "\sim", "\textup{ev}_0"'] \arrow[d, two heads, "\sim"', "\textup{ev}_1"] \\ & A & &  & B
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
in the $\infty$-cosmos.


Let $f \colon A \xrightarrow{{\smash{\mathlower{0.8}{\sim}}}} B$ be an equivalence equipped with the data described in the statement of Lemma \ref{lem:equiv-htpy-equiv} and consider a retract diagram
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} C \arrow[r, "u"'] \arrow[rr, bend left, equals] \arrow[d, "h"'] & A \arrow[d, "\sim", "f"'] \arrow[r, "v"'] & C \arrow[d, "h"] \\ D \arrow[r, "s"] \arrow[rr, bend right, equals] & B \arrow[r, "t"] & D
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
By Lemma \ref{lem:equiv-htpy-equiv}, to prove that $h \colon C \to D$ is an equivalence, it suffices to construct the data of an inverse homotopy equivalence. To that end define $k \colon D \to C$ to be the composite $vgs$ and then observe from the commutative diagrams
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
& & & &[-3pt] & & C \arrow[dr, "h"] \\
& & A \arrow[r, "v"'] &C & &A \arrow[r, "f"'] \arrow[ur, "v"'] & B \arrow[r, "t"] & D \\ C \arrow[r, "u"] \arrow[d, "h"'] \arrow[urrr, bend left=40, equals] & A \arrow[d, "f"'] \arrow[r, "\alpha"] \arrow[ur, equals] & A^{\BbbI} \arrow[u,
"\textup{ev}_0"'] \arrow[d, "\textup{ev}_1"] \arrow[r, "{v^{\BbbI}}"] & C^{\BbbI} \arrow[u, "\textup{ev}_0"'] \arrow[d, "\textup{ev}_1"] & D \arrow[r, "s"] \arrow[drrr, equals,  bend right=40] \arrow[uurr, bend left=30, "k"] & B\arrow[u, "g"'] \arrow[r, "\beta"] \arrow[dr, equals] & B^{\BbbI} \arrow[r, "t^{\BbbI}"] \arrow[d, "\textup{ev}_1"]\arrow[u, "\textup{ev}_0"'] & D^{\BbbI} \arrow[d, "\textup{ev}_1"] \arrow[u, "\textup{ev}_0"'] \\ D \arrow[r, "s"] \arrow[rrr, bend right=20, "k"'] & B \arrow[r, "g"] & A \arrow[r, "v"]  & C & & & B \arrow[r, "t"] & D
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
that $v^{\BbbI} \alpha u \colon C \to C^{\BbbI}$ and $t^{\BbbI} \beta s \colon D \to D^{\BbbI}$ define the required homotopy coherent isomorphisms.

Via Lemma \ref{lem:equiv-htpy-equiv}, the 2-of-3 property for equivalences follows from the fact that the set of  isomorphisms in a quasi-category is closed under composition. Homotopy coherent isomorphisms in a quasi-category represent isomorphisms in the homotopy category, whose composite in the homotopy category is then an isomorphism, which can be lifted to a representing homotopy coherent isomorphism by Proposition \ref{prop:coherent-iso}. We now apply this to the homotopy coherent isomorphisms in the functor spaces of an $\infty$-cosmos that form part of the data of an equivalence of $\infty$-categories.

 To prove that equivalences are closed under composition, consider a composable pair of equivalences with their inverse equivalences
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} A \arrow[r, shift left=.5em, "\sim"', "f"] & B \arrow[r, shift left=.5em, "\sim"', "g"] \arrow[l, shift left=.5em, "k", "\sim"'] & C \arrow[l, shift left=.5em, "\sim"', "h"] \end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
The equivalence data of Lemma \ref{lem:equiv-htpy-equiv} defines isomorphisms $\alpha\colon \textup{id}_A \cong kf \in \qop{Fun}(A,A)$ and $\gamma \colon \textup{id}_B \cong hg \in \qop{Fun}(B,B)$, the latter of which whiskers to define $k\gamma f \colon kf \cong khgf \in \qop{Fun}(B,B)$. Composing these, we obtain an isomorphism $\textup{id}_A \cong khgf \in \qop{Fun}(A,A)$, witnessing that $kh$ defines a left equivalence inverse of $gf$. The other isomorphism is constructed similarly.

To prove that the equivalences are closed under right cancelation,  consider a diagram
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} A \arrow[r, "\sim"', "f"] & B \arrow[r, "g"] \arrow[l, shift left=1em, "k", "\sim"'] & C \arrow[ll, bend right=45, "\sim", "\ell"'] \end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
with $k$ an inverse equivalence to $f$ and $\ell$ and inverse equivalence to $gf$. We claim that $f\ell$ defines an inverse equivalence to $g$. One of the required isomorphisms $\textup{id}_C \cong gf\ell$ is given already. The other is obtained by composing three isomorphisms in $\qop{Fun}(B,B)$
\begin{center} \begin{tikzcd} \textup{id}_B \arrow[r,  "\cong"',  "\beta^{-1}"] & fk \arrow[r, "f\delta k", "\cong"'] & f\ell gf k \arrow[r, "f\ell g\beta", "\cong"'] &f \ell g.\end{tikzcd}\end{center}
The proof of stability of equivalence under left cancelation is dual.

\label{lem:split-triv-fib}

  Every trivial fibration admits a section
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} & E \arrow[d, two heads, "\sim"', "p"] \\ B \arrow[ur, dashed, "s"] \arrow[r, equals] & B
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
that defines a split fiber homotopy equivalence





\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} E \arrow[r, "\alpha"'] \arrow[rr, end anchor = 165, start anchor = 30, bend left=20, "{(\textup{id}_E, sp)}"] \arrow[d, two heads, "p"'] & E^{\BbbI} \arrow[d, two heads, "p^{\BbbI}"'] \arrow[r, two heads, "{(\textup{ev}_0,\textup{ev}_1)}"'] & E \times E \\ B \arrow[r, "\Delta"'] & B^{\BbbI}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
and conversely any isofibration that defines a split fiber homotopy equivalence is a trivial fibration.

If $p \colon E \dhxrightarrow{{\smash{\mathlower{0.8}{\sim}}}} B$ is a trivial fibration, then by the stability property of Lemma \ref{lem:trivial-fib-representability}, so is $p_*\colon\qop{Fun}(X,E)\dhxrightarrow{{\smash{\mathlower{0.8}{\sim}}}} Fun(X,B)$ for any $\infty$-category $X$.  By Definition \ref{defn:qcat-trivial-fibration}, we may solve the lifting problem below-left
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} \emptyset=\partial\Delta[0] \arrow[d, hook] \arrow[r] & \qop{Fun}(B,E) \arrow[d, two heads, "\sim"', "p_*" pos=.4] & {\Bbbone} +{\Bbbone} \arrow[d, hook] \arrow[rr, "{(\textup{id}_E,sp)}"] & & \qop{Fun}(E,E)  \arrow[d, two heads, "\sim"', "p_*" pos=.4] \\ {\Bbbone}= \Delta[0] \arrow[ur, dashed, "s"] \arrow[ur, dashed] \arrow[r, "\textup{id}_B"'] & \qop{Fun}(B,B) & {\BbbI} \arrow[r, "!"'] \arrow[urr, dashed, "\alpha"] & {\Bbbone} \arrow[r, "p"'] & \qop{Fun}(E,B)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
to find a map $s \colon B \to E$ so that $ps = \textup{id}_B$, and then solve the lifting problem above-right to construct the desired fibered homotopy. The converse is immediate from Lemma \ref{lem:equiv-htpy-equiv}.

\label{lem:brown-fact}

  Any functor $f\colon A \to B$ in an $\infty$-cosmos may be factored as an equivalence followed by an isofibration, where this equivalence is constructed as a section of a trivial fibration.
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
  & Pf \arrow[dr, two heads, "p"] \arrow[dl, two heads, bend right, start anchor=190, end anchor=70, "q"'] & \\ A \arrow[rr, "f"'] \arrow[ur, bend right=25, start anchor=30, end anchor=230, "\sim", "s"' pos = 0.6] & & B
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
Moreover, $f$ is an equivalence if and only if the isofibration $p$ is a trivial fibration.

The displayed factorization is constructed by the pullback of an isofibration formed by the simplicial cotensor of the inclusion ${\Bbbone}+{\Bbbone}\hookrightarrow{\BbbI}$ into the $\infty$-category $B$.
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
& A^{\BbbI} \arrow[dr, "f^{\BbbI}"] & \\
A \arrow[r, "\sim", "s"'] \arrow[ur, "\Delta"] \arrow[dr, "{(A,f)}"'] &  Pf \arrow[r] \arrow[d, two heads, "{(q,p)}"' pos=.4] \arrow[dr, phantom, "\lrcorner" very near start] & B^{\BbbI} \arrow[d, two heads, "{(\textup{ev}_0,\textup{ev}_1)}"] \\  & A \times B \arrow[r, "f \times B"'] & B \times B
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
Note the map $q$ is a pullback of the trivial fibration $\textup{ev}_0 \colon B^{\BbbI} \dhxrightarrow{{\smash{\mathlower{0.8}{\sim}}}} B$ and is hence a trivial fibration. Its section $s$, constructed by applying the universal property of the pullback to the displayed cone with summit $A$, is thus an equivalence by the 2-of-3 property. Again by 2-of-3, it follows that $f$ is an equivalence if and only if $p$ is.

\label{rmk:2-of-6}
In fact the equivalences in any $\infty$-cosmos satisfy the stronger \textbf{2-of-6 property}: for any composable triple of functors
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
& B \arrow[dr, "hg", "\sim"'] \\ A \arrow[ur, "f"] \arrow[dr, "gf"',"\sim"] \arrow[rr, "hgf" near start] & & D \\ & C\arrow[from = uu, crossing over, "g" near end] \arrow[ur, "h"']
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
if $gf$ and $hg$ are equivalences then $f$, $g$, $h$, and $hgf$ are too. An argument of Blumberg and Mandell \cite[6.4]{BlumbergMandell:2011ak} uses Lemmas \ref{lem:equivalence-2-of-3}, \ref{lem:split-triv-fib}, and \ref{lem:brown-fact} to prove that the equivalences have the 2-of-6 property.

\label{ex:strong-free} In a cartesian closed category $\ec{V}$, finite products distribute over arbitrary coproducts. In particular, for any sets $X$ and $Y$ there is an isomorphism
\[ \amalg_{X \times Y} 1 \cong (\amalg_X 1) \times (\amalg_Y 1)\] between coproducts of the terminal object $1$, which proves that the functor
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} \mathcal{S}et \arrow[r, "{\amalg_{-} 1}"] & \ec{V}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center} is finite-product-preserving.

\label{prop:change-of-base-adjunction}

  Any adjunction between cartesian closed categories whose left adjoint preserves finite products induces a change-of-base 2-adjunction
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large]
\ec{V} \arrow[r, bend left=20, "F"] \arrow[r, phantom, "\bot"] & \ec{W} \arrow[l, bend left=20, "U"] \arrow[r, phantom, "\rightsquigarrow"] & {\ec{V}}\text{-}\mathcal{C}at \arrow[r, bend left=20, start anchor=10, end anchor=170,  "F_*"] \arrow[r, phantom, "\bot"] & {\ec{W}}\text{-}\mathcal{C}at \arrow[l, bend left=20, start anchor=190, end anchor=-10,  "U_*"]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}

\label{cor:free-underlying-2-adj}

 For any cartesian closed category $\ec{V}$ with coproducts, the underlying category construction and free category construction define  adjoint 2-functors
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large]
  \mathcal{C}at \arrow[r, bend left=20, hook, start anchor=10, end anchor=170] \arrow[r, phantom, "\bot"] & {\ec{V}}\text{-}\mathcal{C}at \arrow[l, bend left=20, start anchor=190, end anchor=-10, "(-)_0"]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}

\label{lem:enriched-cob-adjunction}

Any adjunction comprised of finite-product-preserving functors between cartesian closed categories
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large]
\ec{V} \arrow[r, bend left=20, "F"] \arrow[r, phantom, "\bot"] & \ec{W} \arrow[l, bend left=20, "U"]  \arrow[r, phantom, "\rightsquigarrow"] & \ec{V} \arrow[r, bend left=20, start anchor=20, end anchor=170, "F"] \arrow[r, phantom, "\bot"] & U_*\ec{W} \arrow[l, bend left=20, start anchor=190, end anchor=-15, "U"]  \end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
defines a $\ec{V}$-enriched adjunction between the $\ec{V}$-categories $\ec{V}$ and $U_*\ec{W}$; i.e., there exists a $\ec{V}$-natural isomorphism $U\ec{W}(Fv,w) \cong \ec{V}(v,Uw)$.

The internal action $U_{a,b} \colon U\ec{W}(a,b) \to \ec{V}(Ua,Ub)$ of the $\ec{V}$-functor $U \colon U_*\ec{W} \to \ec{V}$ is defined by the transpose of the map $U\textup{ev} \colon U\ec{W}(a,b) \times Ua \to Ub$ defined by applying $U$ to the counit of the cartesian closure adjunction of $\ec{W}$. The $\ec{V}$-functoriality of this map follows from naturality of evaluation in a cartesian closed category.



By the $\ec{V}$-functoriality of $U \colon U_*\ec{W} \to \ec{V}$, the map
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} U\ec{W}(Fv,w) \arrow[r, "U_{Fv,w}"] & \ec{V}(UFv,Uw) \arrow[r, "{- \circ \eta_v}"] & \ec{V}(v,Uw)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
is $\ec{V}$-natural in $w \in U_*\ec{W}$ for all $v \in \ec{V}$. By a general result about enriching adjoints,  
to construct a compatible $\ec{V}$-enrichment of $F$, we need only demonstrate that this map in an isomorphism in $\ec{V}$.

We do this by constructing an explicit inverse, namely
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
\ec{V}(v,Uw) \arrow[r, "\eta"] & UF\ec{V}(v,Uw) \arrow[r, "U(F_{v,Uw})"] & U\ec{W}(Fv,FUw) \arrow[r, "\epsilon_w \circ -"] & U\ec{W}(Fv,w)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
where the middle map is defined by applying the unenriched functor $U$ to the action map from the $\ec{W}$-functor $F \colon F_*\ec{V} \to \ec{W}$, which is defined similarly to the $\ec{V}$-functor $U \colon U_*\ec{W} \to \ec{V}$.

The proof that these maps are inverses involves a pair of diagram chases, the first of which demonstrates that the top-right composite reduces to the left-bottom composite, which is the identity:
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
\ec{V}(v,Uw) \arrow[r, "\eta"] \arrow[ddrr, "\eta_{Uw}\circ-"'] & UF\ec{V}(v,Uw) \arrow[r, "U(F_{v,Uw})"] \arrow[dr, "UF_{v, Uw}" description] & U\ec{W}(Fv,FUw) \arrow[r, "\epsilon_w \circ -"] \arrow[d, "U_{Fv,FUw}"] & U\ec{W}(Fv,w) \arrow[d, "U_{Fv,w}"] \\ & & \ec{V}(UFv,UFUw) \arrow[r, "U\epsilon_w \circ -"]  \arrow[d, "-\circ \eta_v"]& \ec{V}(UFv,Uw) \arrow[d, "-\circ \eta_v"] \\ & & \ec{V}(v,UFUw) \arrow[r, "U\epsilon_w\circ-"] & \ec{V}(v,Uw)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
The only subtle point is the commutativity of the trapezoidal region, which expresses the fact that $\eta \colon \textup{id}_{\ec{V}} \Rightarrow UF$ is a \emph{closed natural transformation} between product-preserving functors between cartesian closed categories. This region commutes because the transposed diagram does:
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd} \ec{V}(v,Uw) \times v\arrow[d, "\eta \times \eta_v"'] \arrow[r, equals] &  \ec{V}(v,Uw) \times v \arrow[d, "\eta"']  \arrow[r, "\textup{ev}"] & Uw \arrow[d, "\eta_{Uw}"] \\  UF\ec{V}(v,Uw)\times UFv  \arrow[r, "\cong"] & UF(\ec{V}(v,UW)\times v) \arrow[r, "UF\textup{ev}"] & UFUw
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
the right-hand square by naturality, and the left-hand square because any naturally transformation between product-preserving functors is automatically a monoidal natural transformation. 
  The other diagram chase is similar.

\label{prop:cob-adjunction}

Given an adjunction between cartesian closed categories
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep=large]
\ec{V} \arrow[r, bend left=20, "F"] \arrow[r, phantom, "\bot"] & \ec{W} \arrow[l, bend left=20, "U"]   \end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
whose left adjoint preserves finite products then if $\ec{C}$ is co/tensored as a $\ec{W}$-cat\-e\-gory,  $U_*\ec{C}$ is co/tensored as $\ec{V}$-category with the co/tensor of $c \in \ec{C}$ by $v \in \ec{V}$ defined by
\[ v \otimes c := Fv \otimes c \qquad \text{and} \qquad c^v := c^{Fv}.\]

Suppose $\ec{C}$ admits cotensors as a $\ec{W}$-category. To verify that $U_*\ec{C}$ admits cotensors as a $\ec{V}$-category we must supply an isomorphism
\[ U\ec{C}(x,c^{Fv}) \cong (U\ec{C}(x,c))^v\] in $\ec{V}$ that is $\ec{V}$-natural in $x$. By the enriched Yoneda lemma, we can extract this isomorphism from an isomorphism \[ \ec{V}(u,U\ec{C}(x,c^{Fv})) \cong \ec{V}(u,(U\ec{C}(x,c))^v)\] that is $\ec{V}$-natural in $u \in \ec{V}$. To that end, by composing the $\ec{V}$-natural isomorphisms of Lemma \ref{lem:enriched-cob-adjunction}, the enriched natural isomorphisms arising from the cartesian closed structure on $\ec{V}$ and on $U_*\ec{W}$, and the isomorphisms that characterize the cotensor on $\ec{C}$ and express the fact that $F$ preserves binary products, we have:
\begin{align*} \ec{V}(u,U\ec{C}(x,c^{Fv})) &\cong U\ec{W}(Fu, \ec{C}(x,c^{Fv})) \cong U\ec{W}(Fu, \ec{C}(x,c)^{Fv}) \\ &\cong U\ec{W}(Fu \times Fv, \ec{C}(x,c))  \cong U\ec{W}(F(u \times v), \ec{C}(x,c)) \\ &\cong \ec{V}(u \times v, U\ec{C}(x,c)) \cong \ec{V}(u, (U\ec{C}(x,c))^v). \qedhere\end{align*}

